<style>
/* The box is tight around the `DREW'S` / `30` / `B'DAY` stack, and the `TH`
   overhang paints outside it. That is what keeps the stack optically centred:
   widening the viewBox to hold the overhang would make the browser centre a box
   whose mass sits left of its centre, which is the misalignment this avoids.
   Nothing on the hero clips the overhang, and it stays inside the page's side
   padding at every size the rules below allow.

   Sized against both viewport axes, because the height follows from the width
   through the viewBox ratio: `50vw` carries a phone, `17.5rem` stops the stack
   growing past the heading it replaces on a desktop, and `56svh` is the ceiling
   the resulting height has to respect. The short-viewport rule steps all three
   down, because there the runner band, the eyebrow and the subtitle leave the
   least room above the fold. */
.title-art {
	width: min(50vw, 17.5rem, 56svh);
	height: auto;
	overflow: visible;
}

/* Print sizing is the poster's to decide: it sets the width on the lockup's
   wrapper and the art fills it. The `TH` overhang still needs room, which the
   poster's side padding provides. */
.title-art-print {
	width: 100%;
	max-width: none;
}

@media (max-height: 639px) {
	.title-art:not(.title-art-print) {
		width: min(46vw, 14.5rem, 37svh);
	}
}
</style>
